<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Catalog.App</RootNamespace>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <!--
      JWT bearer validation. The catalog validates the token the identity service signs; without it
      the gateway forwards a credential nobody checks and every endpoint is effectively public.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Nur.Tricenter.Security" />
    <!-- Permission NAMES come from the shared kernel so identity can grant them without
         depending on this service. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Nur.Tricenter.Access" />
    <PackageReference Include="Nur.Tricenter.Integration" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" />
    <!--
      Health checks. /health is the endpoint this service advertises to Consul (see
      Catalog.Infrastructure/Consul/ServiceRegistration), so it is a runtime dependency of service
      discovery, not a nice-to-have dashboard.

      The Npgsql check is registered ONLY when ServiceRegistration:ServiceDiscoveryAddress is
      configured, in Startup.ConfigureServices. That is deliberate and load-bearing: the integration
      suite boots the real host through WebApplicationFactory with the EF Core InMemory provider,
      and an unconditional Npgsql check would make every one of those tests open a TCP connection to
      a database that is not there. Gating it on the same predicate that gates Consul registration
      means a host with no discovery configuration reports Healthy from having no checks, instead of
      reporting Unhealthy because of a check it should never have had.

      The /health-ui DASHBOARD (AspNetCore.HealthChecks.UI + .UI.InMemory.Storage) is deliberately
      NOT installed, despite being offered. Verified against a real host, not assumed: the UI's
      InMemory storage drags Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory 8.0.11 into a net10 app whose
      EF Core resolves to 10.0.0-preview.2, and its UIInitializationHostedService then throws
      "TypeLoadException: Method 'TranslateRightJoin' ... does not have an implementation", which
      fails Host.StartAsync outright. A cosmetic dashboard that takes the service down on boot is
      worse than no dashboard, so /health stays as the machine-facing contract and the UI is left
      out. Revisit only on a UI release built against EF Core 10.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.NpgSql" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Catalog.Infrastructure\Catalog.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <!--
      The public contract project: CatalogPermissions (one entry per authorization policy),
      CatalogRoutes (the route table the gateway aggregates) and the integration messages.
      The App needs it to register policies and to annotate controllers; without this reference
      the contract files were compiled nowhere reachable from the host.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\contracts\Catalog.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>